From cd1bcf633e0d575600463ce88c14a6e5dc5f8001 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alan Rockefeller <alanrockefeller@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 21:28:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] Parsimony-biased moves: exact AVX2 Fitch passes and join
 lengths

ParsSPR and ParsTBR (6 of the 52 default proposal weights for a non-clock
GTR+G analysis) score every candidate regraft position by parsimony, and
GetFitchPartials / GetParsFP recompute the down- and final-pass state sets
over the whole tree first. All of it ran one site at a time with a
data-dependent branch per site.

dikarya.h adds AVX2 versions (4 sites per instruction) used when the CPU has
AVX2 and MRBAYES_DIKARYA_SCALAR is unset:
  - DkFitchDown (GetFitchPartials), DkFitchFinal (GetParsFP): the same
    bitwise set algebra, lane by lane, selected with compare masks;
  - DkParsJoinLength: sum of site weights where ((x1|x2) & (y1|y2)) == 0,
    covering every form used by Move_ParsSPR1 and Move_ParsTBR1.

Exactness: the set operations are exact by nature. The original loops add
float site weights one by one into a double. When every weight is a whole
number in [0, 2^24] -- pattern counts always are, unless reweighting is
switched on -- each partial sum is an integer below 2^53, every addition
is exact, and the total is the integer sum whatever the order; the fast
paths check that condition (per call for the Fitch pass, once per move for
the join lengths) and otherwise run the original loop.

The scalar loops stay for two BitsLongs per site (more than 64 states).

Output of complete MrBayes runs is byte-identical with and without this
change (stdout timing lines aside) on 44 real alignments x 2 model/chain
settings. On a 36-taxon ITS alignment the four functions took 12% of the
run before and under 3% after.
