From 37d317d28f05bbca4dac79d5f1a1c9c10e3f063e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alan Rockefeller <alanrockefeller@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 03:05:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Concurrent chains: each generation's likelihoods computed
 on N threads

With `set beaglethreadcount=N` (N >= 2), a run that qualifies computes the
chains' likelihoods on N threads, a chain per thread at a time, instead of one
chain after the other. It qualifies with one division on BEAGLE's
single-precision 4-state CPU code from BEAGLE 4.0.1-dikarya2 or later (whose
instances keep per-thread scratch), dynamic scaling, two or more chains, only
the moves Dikarya's analyses use (DkMoveAllowed), and no Gibbs sampling,
autocorrelated gamma, ancestral-state or site-rate reports, stepping-stone
sampling, character reweighting or appending. Otherwise nothing changes, and
BEAGLE splits large alignments by pattern as before.

Why it is the same run:

- Everything that uses random numbers stays on the main thread in the
  original order: each chain's move, then its acceptance draw (in a BEAGLE run
  nothing between the two uses random numbers or can abort the move), then the
  swaps. A swap draws exactly one number from the main stream whatever its
  outcome, so its place in the stream is reserved and the swap attempted as
  soon as its run's chains are done; the next moves of that run follow while
  the other run's likelihoods are computed. No move reads another run's state.
- Each chain has its own scratch buffers (conditional likelihoods, transition
  matrices, node scalers, eigen systems) instead of the one set MrBayes hands
  from chain to chain, and its likelihood runs on a private copy of the
  division's ModelInfo: it reads only that chain's trees and parameters and
  writes only its own buffers. Same operations, same operands.
- The site-scaler scratch and its scaler-node flags stay shared, as in
  MrBayes: ResetScalersPartition leaves some flags as it finds them and they
  are then read, so which chain flipped the array last matters. Numerical
  rescaling, the only thing that flips them, is done on the main thread at
  the chain's acceptance step, in chain order (DkBeagleRescaleAll).
- The division flags the chains share (update flags, rescale state) are set at
  each acceptance step to what the original loop leaves there after that
  chain's likelihood, so ResetFlips and recalculateScalers see what they did.
- The debug build's prior check, a function of the chain's own state, is
  computed with its likelihood; its message, like every other, is printed by
  the main thread where the original prints it.

Two threads are not always faster: next to another busy process they keep
waiting for each other (a generation waits for its slowest chain) and lost
~15% to one thread. So the run measures its generations per second each way,
over stretches of at least 0.2 s between output events, averages the
measurements of each way, uses the other way when it is over 5% faster, and
tries it again every 64 stretches. On its own the main thread passes a single
scratch set from chain to chain as MrBayes does: per-chain sets cost ~4% in
cache footprint. Which thread computes a likelihood changes nothing in it.

Verified identical to stock MrBayes 3.2.9 + BEAGLE 4.0.1 (same input, same
seed, `set beaglethreadcount=2` before the seed in both) on 44 real alignments
x 2 model/chain settings, ThreadSanitizer-clean, and stopping on the same NaN
at the same generation as stock on the two real jobs that do. Two threads
against one on this 2-vCPU host, Dikarya's settings, quiet host: 1.58x on a
36-taxon ITS job, 1.59x on 129 taxa, 1.53x on 134, 1.41x on 240 taxa x 564
patterns and on 188 x 1191 patterns.
